Date menu: the calendar panel

The events list reads the calendar server and lists what is coming up from
now into next month, grouped by day (Today, Tomorrow, Sun, Nov 8), at most
four rows and then "N more this week" (or "N more coming up"). Choosing a
day lists that day ("Nothing on Thursday." when it is empty); choosing it
again, or today, goes back to what is coming up. Each row is a button that
opens the event in Luma Calendar (--event-uid=<uid> --event-day=<day>, the
command line Calendar's reminders use); "N more" and Open Calendar open
Calendar on that day (--event-day=<day>).

Rows show the calendar's colour (painted as Calendar paints it: the colour's
hue at the LumaUI mark lightness and chroma), the title, the place under it
and the time.
The calendar server sends each event's location and its calendar's colour in
the extras dictionary it already carried.

The month on show is separate from the chosen day: the arrows page months
without choosing a day, and Today comes back when another month is on show;
the month header replaces the date header (the clock shows the date). Arrow
keys walk the days and page at the month's edge, and the focused day keeps
focus when events arrive. Opening Calendar closes the menu. While a day is
chosen the menu keeps its height and the spare room goes below the list. Each day's accessible
name says how many events it has. The event range asked of the server covers
the days on show and what the list needs.

